{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Behold, I am slaying your firstborn son.</b> According to the divine judgment, which is measure for measure, as it says: וּכְאֹרַח אִישׁ יַמְצִאֶנּוּ (Job 34:11). For indeed the plague of the firstborn alone was a punitive judgment upon Pharaoh, unlike all the other plagues; rather, the rest of the plagues were for a sign and a wonder, in order that they might repent — for He does not desire the death of the dead, since He did not block before them the paths of true repentance at all. Had they been wise enough to return to the blessed El out of love for His goodness and awe at His greatness — which is the repentance that reaches the very throne of glory, that shields and grants favor in the eyes of Elohim, as the Sages of blessed memory said (Yoma 86a): עֲוֹנוֹת נֶחְשָׁבִים לוֹ כִּזְכֻיּוֹת (his sins are accounted to him as merits) — or at least to return as servants out of fear of what He is able to punish. But the plague of the firstborn and the drowning of Pharaoh and his army in the sea were divine judgments to enact measure for measure."], :book :exodus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:24:28.979762490Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}